WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:07.980
SPEAKER: Welcome to
the quickstart tutorial

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.465
for Cloud Functions.

00:00:09.465 --> 00:00:12.090
We're going to see how to deploy
a Cloud Function from a Google

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.890
Cloud project.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.140
To start, make sure
you've got a Google Cloud

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.820
project with building enabled.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.790
The first thing you want to do
is enable Cloud Functions API.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.620
In the Navigation menu,
click on API and Services,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.460
and click Enable
APIs and Services.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:32.670
Here, search for Cloud Functions
and select it from the results.

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.760
On the Cloud Functions
API page, click Enable

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.930
to enable it on the project.

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.960
Next, head to Cloud Functions
on the Navigation menu.

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.710
We'll find this under
the Compute section.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.555
Click here, where it
says Create Function.

00:00:46.555 --> 00:00:48.180
Name your function
whatever you'd like.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.150
For this demonstration,
we'll keep

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.150
HTTP selected as a trigger.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:01.750
Keep Inline Editor selected
for the source code.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.500
As we can see here,
Cloud Functions

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.440
support multiple runtimes.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.260
For now, we'll use Node.js 8.

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.570
By default, we're provided
with a basic hello,

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:14.810
world function that will
respond to an HTTP request.

00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:17.080
Click on the expanded
Editor window icon

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.070
to get a better look.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.820
We won't have to make
any changes here, so

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.900
click OK to go back.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.330
Here, where it says
Function to Execute,

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:28.840
is where we would specify the
function in our source code

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.620
that we'd like to execute.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.770
In this case, hello, world
is the only one that we have,

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.110
and it's the one that
we want to execute.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.030
Click Create to deploy
our Cloud Function.

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:41.800
The Cloud Functions
Overview page

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.250
will give us a general overview
of all of our Cloud Functions,

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.990
including region,
trigger, runtime,

00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:49.780
and the time of its
last deployment,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.130
as well as permissions.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:54.010
Once it's done deploying,
click the menu of our function

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.200
and click Test Function.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:58.420
On the testing page, we
can test our function

00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.560
by sending a triggering
event in JSON format.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.360
Our test function
responds to events

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.470
by outputting the
value for message,

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.800
so let's send it a JSON object
with a value for message.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.940
Click on Test the Function
and check out the output.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:17.430
As we expected, the
function outputs the message

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.540
that we provided above.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:24.140
Down below, we'll see the log
associated with our function.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.020
There are entries for when the
function execution started,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.850
as well as when the
function finished,

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.940
along with status code.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:33.710
We can get an expanded
view of the logs

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.920
by clicking here, where it says
See All Logs for This Function

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.850
Execution.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.610
Here we'll see the same log
entries, along with a lot more

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:44.270
information should we need it.

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.900
And that's all there is to it.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.950
We've successfully
deployed a Cloud Function.

